export const noteSourceById = {"java-dev-environment":"<!-- 29/12/2025 -->\n\n# **Setting Up Development Environment**\n\nTo learn Java, we need 2 things:\n  1. Java Development Kit(JDK)\n  2. Code Editor\n\nJDK stands for Java development kit and it's basically a `software development environment` for building Java applications.\n\nIt has a :-\n  1. compiler\n  2. a bunch of code that we can reuse\n  3. a Java runtime environment (JRE)\n  4. bunch of other stuff.\n\nChoose Java SE, which is short for Java standard edition.\n\nFor IDE we can choose among these popular ones:-\n  1. netbeans\n  2. eclipse\n  3. intelliJ\n  \nI would recommend to use intelliJ community edition because it's free. Later on, use vscode due to no need to pay when want to use javaspringboot.\n\nDownload both JDK and preferred IDE from their official website.","java-anatomy":"<!-- 7/1/2026 -->\n\n# **Anatomy of a Java Program**\n\nJava program comprises of functions. Function is a block of code that performs a task such as:-\n\n1. sending emails to people,\n2. converting someone's weight in pounds to kilograms,\n3. validating users input and so on.\n\nWe start by specifying the **return type** of that function.\n\n```java\n\nReturnType Name() {\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nSome functions return a value like a number, a date, time and so on. Other functions don't return anything. So the return type of this function is **void**.\n\n```java\n\nvoid Name() {\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nGive function a proper descriptive name, like send email.\n\n\n```java\n\nvoid sendEmail() {\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nAdd the parameters for this function. We use these parameters to pass values to our function.\n\nAfter the parentheses, we add a pair of curly braces and inside this braces, we write the actual Java code.\n\nNow every Java program should have at least one function and that function is called **main**.\n\n```java\n\nvoid main() {\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nSo `main` is the entry point to our programs.\nWhenever we execute a Java program, the main function gets called and the code inside this function gets executed.\n\nNow this functions don't exist on their own. They should always belong to a class. So a class is a **_container for one or more related functions_**. Basically, we use these classes to organize our code. A class in Java contains related functions.\n\nNow every Java program should have at least one class that contains the main function. It's called main class. We start with a class keyword, then we give our class a proper descriptive name. And then we add a pair of curly braces. Now the functions that we define in between these curly braces belong to this class.\n\n```java\nclass Main {\n    void main() {\n     ...\n    }\n}\n```\nAnd more accurately, we refer to them as **methods**. So a method is a function that is part of a class. In some program languages like **Python**, we can have a function that exists outside of the class. So we call it a function. But when a function belongs to a class, we refer to it as a method of that class.\n\nPackage is collection of classes, classes is collection of methods.\n```java\npackage\n    ->\n        classes\n            -> \n                methods\n```\nNow, in Java, all these classes and methods should have an **access modifier**. An access modifier is a special keyword that determines if other classes and methods in this program can access these classes and methods. We have various access modifiers like public, private, and so on.\n\nNow most of the time, we use the public access modifier.\nSo we put that in front of our class and method declarations.\n\nSo this is the basic structure of a Java program. At a minimum, we have a main class and inside this main class, we have the main method.\n\n<!-- ![Anatomy of a Java Program](/api/drive-image/1bAOSno2ylLfr1wOX8hPTvZYfwkWq75GP) -->\n```java\npublic class Main {\n    public void main() {\n     ...\n    }\n}\n```\n\n\nTo name our **classes**, we use the `Pascal naming convention`.\nThat basically means the first letter of every word should be uppercase.\n\nIn contrast to name our `methods`, we use the `camel naming convention`.\nAnd that means the first letter of every word should be uppercase except the first word.","first-java-program":"<!-- 14/1/2026 -->\n\n# **First Java Program**\n\n- Double slash `//` is for comments.\n\n- For now, just remember that the main method in our program should always be static. Example given public `static` void.\n\n- void means this method is not going to return any value.\n\n```java\npackage dev.syakir;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main (String [] args) {\n        System.out.println(\"Hello World\");\n    }\n}\n\nwill be printed Hello World on the terminal.\n```","how-java-code-gets-executed":"<!-- 14/1/2026 -->\n\n# **How Java Code Gets Executed**\n\nJava program runs in 2 steps, compilation and execution. \n\nIn the compilation step, IntelliJ uses the Java compiler to compile our code into a different\nformat called Java byte code. This Java compiler comes with the Java development kit\nthat we downloaded before this.\n\nNow we can invoke the Java compiler by using terminal and command `javac` and pass the name\nor Java file as an argument so it would be like this:-\n\n```terminal\nmacpc $ javac Main.java\n```\n\nMake sure to a spell correct with a capital M because these operating systems are case sensitive.\n\nThen we type `ls` to take a look what's inside of this folder. \n\n```\n//terminal\nmacpc $javac Main.java\nmacpc $ls\nMain.class Main.java\nmacpc $cd ../..\nsrc $java dev.syakir.Main\nHello World\nsrc $\n```\nNow we have a new file that is Main.class. This is the bytecode representation of this Java file.That file is stored within production folder. So this was the compilation step. When we run a program using IntelliJ, all these steps are hidden from us.\nWe don't see the compilation or execution steps.\n\nThis Java bytecode that we have\nin this file is platform independent. And that means it can run on Windows, Mac, Linux, or\nany operating systems that has a Java runtime environment. If we go to Java.com/download,\nwe can download Java or more accurately Java runtime environment for various operating\nsystems. This Java runtime environment has a software component called Java virtual machine\nor JVM. This JVM takes our Java bytecode and translates it to the native code for the\nunderlying operating system. \n\nSo if you're on a Windows machine, this Java virtual machine\nconverts our Java bytecode into the native code that Windows can understand. This architecture\nis the reason why Java applications are portable or platform independent. We can write\na Java program on a Windows machine and execute it on Linux, Mac, or any other operating\nsystems that have a Java runtime environment. C sharp and Python also have the same architecture.\nThat's why they're platform independent as well.","5_interesting_facts_about_java":"<!-- 16/1/2026 -->\n\n#  **5 Interesting Facts About Java**\n\n1. Java was developed by James Gosling in 1995 at Sun Microsystems, which was later acquired\nby Oracle in 2010. It was originally called Oak after an oak tree that stood outside\nGosling's office. Later it was renamed to Green and was finally renamed to Java, inspired\nby Java Coffee. That's why it's logo looks like a cup of coffee.\n\n2. Have 4 editions of Java for building different kinds of applications:-\n - **_Java standard edition_**. This is the core Java platform. It contains\nall of the libraries that every Java developer must learn. \n - **_Java enterprise edition_**, which is used for building very large scale and distributed systems. It's built\non top of Java standard edition and provides additional libraries for building fault\ntolerant, distributed, multi-tier software. \n - **_Java micro edition_**, which is a subset of Java standard edition designed for mobile devices. So it has libraries, specific\nto mobile devices. \n - **_Java card_**, which is used in smart cards. The latest version of Java is Java standard edition 12, which was released just a few months ago in March 2019. \n \n3. Java has closed to 9 million developers worldwide. \n\n4. Currently about 3 billions mobile phones run Java as well as 120 millions TV sets and every blue ray player. \n\n5. According to indeed.com, the average salary of a Java developer is just over $100,000 per year in\nthe US. So as we can see, Java is everywhere, which means more opportunities to get hired as a professional Java programmer.\n\n*data as of 2019","java-types-introduction":"","java-types-variables":"<!-- 19/1/2026 -->\n\n# **Variables**\n\nWe use variables to temporarily store data in computer's memory.\n\nFor example we want to store someone's age in the memory.\nSo we declare a variable like this.\nint age equals 30.\n\n```java\nint age = 30;\n```\nSo int or integer is the type of this variable.\nThis variable we can only store integers which are whole numbers.\nLike 1, 2, 3, 4, numbers that don't have a decimal point.\n\n1. First we specify the type of variable.\n2. Then we give it a name or a label. This is also called an identifier. Because we use it to identify our variable.\n3. The equals sign is called the assignment operator. And 30 is the initial value that we are assigning to this variable.\n\nWe're initializing this variable which means we're giving it an initial value.\nWe always have to initialize our variables before reading them.\n\nSo with this line, we're storing number 30 somewhere in computer's memory.\nAnd we're assigning this label to that memory location.\n\nWe can also change the value of our variable.\nSo after we initialize it.\nWe can change it to 35.\n\nNow when we run this program again.\nWe see 35.\n\nWe can also initialize multiple variables on the same line.\nBut this is something that not recommended because it makes our code ugly and hard to read.\n\nWe can also copy the value of one variable into another.\n\nLet me delete these variables.\nAnd declare another variable like temperature and set it to 20.\nSo using a comma we can declare multiple variables on the same line.\nNow even though this is technically possible.\nIt's not something that is recommended.\n\nSo it's always better to declare one variable on each line like this.\n\nWe can also copy the value of one variable into another.\n\nWe can declare a new variable called my age.\nWe set it to 30.\nAnd then we declare another variable like her age.\nAnd we set it to my age.\n\nWe're going to see 30.\n\nSo we're copying the value of this variable into this other variable.","java-types-primitive-types":"# Primitive Types\n\nComing soon.","java-types-reference-types":"# Reference Types\n\nComing soon.","java-types-primitive-vs-reference":"# Primitive vs Reference Types\n\nComing soon.","java-types-strings":"# Strings\n\nComing soon.","java-types-escape-sequences":"# Escape Sequences\n\nComing soon.","java-types-arrays":"# Arrays\n\nComing soon.","java-types-multi-dimensional-arrays":"# Multi-dimensional Arrays\n\nComing soon.","java-types-constants":"# Constants\n\nComing soon.","java-types-arithmetic-expressions":"# Arithmetic Expressions\n\nComing soon.","java-types-order-of-operations":"# Order Of Operations\n\nComing soon.","java-types-casting":"# Casting\n\nComing soon.","java-types-the-math-class":"# The Math Class\n\nComing soon.","java-types-formatting-numbers":"# Formatting Numbers\n\nComing soon.","java-types-reading-input":"# Reading Input\n\nComing soon.","java-types-project-mortgage-calculator":"# Project: Mortgage Calculator\n\nComing soon.","java-control-flow-introduction":"# Introduction\n\nComing soon.","java-control-flow-comparison-operators":"# Comparison Operators\n\nComing soon.","java-control-flow-logical-operators":"# Logical Operators\n\nComing soon.","java-control-flow-if-statements":"# If Statements\n\nComing soon.","java-control-flow-simplifying-if-statements":"# Simplifying If Statements\n\nComing soon.","java-control-flow-ternary-operator":"# The Ternary Operator\n\nComing soon.","java-control-flow-switch-statements":"# Switch Statements\n\nComing soon.","java-control-flow-exercise-fizzbuzz":"# Exercise: FizzBuzz\n\nComing soon.","java-control-flow-for-loops":"# For Loops\n\nComing soon.","java-control-flow-while-loops":"# While Loops\n\nComing soon.","java-control-flow-do-while-loops":"# Do While Loops\n\nComing soon.","java-control-flow-break-and-continue":"# Break and Continue Statements\n\nComing soon.","java-control-flow-for-each-loop":"# For-Each Loop\n\nComing soon.","java-control-flow-project-mortgage-calculator":"# Project: Mortgage Calculator\n\nComing soon.","java-control-flow-solution":"# Solution\n\nComing soon.","java-control-flow-summary":"# Summary\n\nComing soon.","java-clean-coding-introduction":"# Introduction\n\nComing soon.","java-clean-coding-clean-coding":"# Clean Coding\n\nComing soon.","java-clean-coding-creating-methods":"# Creating Methods\n\nComing soon.","java-clean-coding-refactoring":"# Refactoring\n\nComing soon.","java-clean-coding-extracting-methods":"# Extracting Methods\n\nComing soon.","java-clean-coding-refactoring-repetitive-patterns":"# Refactoring Repetitive Patterns\n\nComing soon.","java-clean-coding-project-payment-schedule":"# Project: Payment Schedule\n\nComing soon.","java-clean-coding-solution":"# Solution\n\nComing soon.","java-clean-coding-refactoring-the-code":"# Refactoring the Code\n\nComing soon.","java-clean-coding-summary":"# Summary\n\nComing soon.","java-debugging-introduction":"# Introduction\n\nComing soon.","java-debugging-types-of-errors":"# Types of Errors\n\nComing soon.","java-debugging-common-syntax-errors":"# Common Syntax Errors\n\nComing soon.","java-debugging-debugging-java-applications":"# Debugging Java Applications\n\nComing soon.","java-debugging-packaging-java-applications":"# Packaging Java Applications\n\nComing soon.","java-debugging-course-wrap-up":"# Course Wrap Up\n\nComing soon."} as const;
